    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S939A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the residential\ncare off-site facility demonstration project\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo establish a demonstration project allowing for the provision of\nphysical, occupational and speech therapy by a residential health care\nfacility at an off-site location.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one adds a new Section 2822 to the Public Health Law to\nestablish the \"Residential care off-site facility demonstration\nproject.\" Under the demonstration project, the Department of Health\nwould approve up to three residential health care facilities to\nprovide physical, occupational and speech therapy, as well as related\neducational services, at an off-site facility as a means to assess\nreimbursement methodologies and delivery methods related to such\nservices.\n\nProviders eligible to participate in the demonstration project must be\nissued a RHCF operating certificate, along with a certificate to\nprovide outpatient services at its main location.\n\nSection two establishes an immediate effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nExisting law would require residential health care facilities to\nundergo a complete certificate of need process to operate an off-site\nfacility.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nDemonstration programs allow the State to test innovative approaches\nto care delivery that would often be impossible or subject to\nburdensome administrative requirements. In this case, provision of\nphysical therapy and other services outside of a nursing home will\nbroaden the range of care and treatment residents are able to access.\nFor example, a partnership between the RHCF and a non-profit health\nclub or recreation facility would allow physical therapists to use\nequipment such as weight machines and Swimming pools. This would allow\nthe therapists to use methods that may speed recovery and improve the\nquality of life for residents who would otherwise not have the\nopportunity to access to such comprehensive care. Speedier recoveries\nand maintenance of a generally higher level of health among nursing\nhome residents may in turn save the State money since an overwhelming\nproportion of long term care in that setting is government-financed.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 Passed Senate\n2008 Vetoed by Governor\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
